WEBVTT

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:10.610
Eyes.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.400
To be stunned.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.750
To don a serious visage.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:14.870
To be partial to someone.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.210
To keep an eye on something.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.610
Our eyes are organs capable of
passively receiving visual information.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:31.080
At times, they are also organs that actively convey unspoken intent, emotions, and inner thoughts.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.200
Eyes can say just as much as one's mouth.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.140
This is a fitting and accurate way to describe them.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.070
That man... if I remember correctly the last time he visited...

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.440
Yeah. He's our homeroom teacher.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.850
I wonder why he stopped by.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.390
Hotori probably caused trouble again.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.270
Sensei, can I ask you something?

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.920
What is it, Arashiyama?

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.000
You came all this way to see me after school...

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.700
Did you fall for me?

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:06.960
What?

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:41.500
Huh?

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:42.550
What!?

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.070
It's not adding up!?

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.330
No pulse! Heartbeat, breathing, blood pressure... all zero!

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:53.150
Oh, you're a bachelor, Sensei?

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.120
And you're so handsome! What a shame!

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.440
He has to make his own lunch. He must be lonely...

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.420
It ends up in my stomach either way.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.470
Take off! Quit flirting with him!

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.270
You don't own the customer, ya know.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.460
I saw him first!

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:12.790
So,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.080
What do you want with me anyway?

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.910
If it's extracurricular classes, count me out.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.660
I came across something strange
when dividing my grandfather's estate.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:23.690
He just ignored my flirting?

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:32.920
This is it.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:35.440
A portrait?

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:36.410
It's a portrait, right?

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.520
Looks like an ordinary portrait to me.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.730
What's so strange about it?

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:42.310
Oh,

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.700
This is a pretty odd painting.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:45.460
What?

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:51.240
We all just said it was normal, so
why would you be the only one to say it's not?

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.500
You're just trying to steer the
conversation back to you! You uppity little—

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.480
That's not it! Okay fine, maybe a little, but still...

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.500
I knew it!

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:01.590
Look at it.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.210
The canvas is a perfect square!

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.170
Portraits are usually taller or wider, right?

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:09.340
She's got a point.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.070
Now that you mention it...

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:16.560
I see. This portrait does have a 1:1 ratio. In other words, a perfect square.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.930
Well done, Arashiyama.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:23.460
I brought this here because I knew you had an eye for the extraordinary.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.520
You're a very special person to me.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.620
T-That's... but I'm still in high school!

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.550
Again with the selective hearing...

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.600
These were done by my grandfather.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.830
The last of three children, he was an unsuccessful painter.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.040
He was also a gambler. He didn't leave much behind,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.550
But he did amass quite a few paintings during his lifetime.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:48.730
After his death,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:54.060
They were split between myself and his two siblings.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:58.250
But the real issue lies with the other portrait!

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:06.390
What the heck is that?

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.340
He's got four eyes!

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.370
It's creeping me out!

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.010
According to the labels on the back, I believe these are self portraits.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.280
And the titles for both portraits are the same:

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:16.250
“Eyes”.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:17.520
“Eyes”?

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.380
Why would my grandfather paint something so bizarre?

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.540
Ever since I laid eyes upon it, it's bugged me to no end.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.900
My heart is tormented with confusion!

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.060
As you know, I am a math teacher.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.110
My job is to view things in a linear and logical fashion.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.920
What went through his head as he drew this?

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.050
Ha! Nonsense!

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.540
I'm not him, so how could I possibly figure it out!?

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.660
Ahh, he's gone crazy!

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:53.140
But with the right perspective, it
should be possible to produce a hypothesis.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.840
Arashiyama, you like detective novels, right?

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.480
Yes! Love them!

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:03.030
That's why I want you to solve the mystery of this painting.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:03.780
Me?

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:08.450
You're to present me with an acceptable answer.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.210
I'll come back tomorrow.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.630
Sheesh. What a troubled man.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.490
On the other hand, it may be because of his
difficult personality that ladies don't flock to him.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.730
Better bag him while you've got the chance, Hotori!

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.430
Enough about him. You're disturbing my thinking!

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:37.870
You still haven't got it?

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.080
I've already figured it out.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:40.680
What!?

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.450
I solved the riddle too.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:43.420
Even you, Granny!?

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:46.430
Care to hear my reasoning?

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.060
Do you know who painted this portrait?

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:53.050
Yeah, that's—

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.160
Post-impressionist painter Vincent van Gogh.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:03.700
It's said that he painted using such unique colors
and styles because that's how the world appeared to him.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:10.270
Likewise, it follows that this
man also painted reality as he saw it.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:10.710
Huh?

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:14.940
Ergo, Sensei's grandfather had astigmatism!

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.610
Oh, that's some nice detective work there.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:22.760
But how come he only drew one nose and mouth on each portrait?

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.890
Head Maid, this girl's pissing me off!

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:27.680
The other one's perfectly normal anyway.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.470
It doesn't make sense that there are a different amount of eyes each time!

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:31.510
You're too naïve, Hotori.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.950
In that case, think of it this way.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:39.760
Sensei's grandfather left behind many paintings.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:44.230
And the fact that Sensei found it odd

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:47.310
means that there were others that he didn't find weird.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.470
Like landscapes, still-lifes, and so on.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:54.930
Out of all those ordinary paintings, only that one was strange.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.830
Yeah, I guess so.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:02.760
He was a man who only drew ordinary pictures.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.810
If you think of it like that...

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.670
Instead of thinking this portrait is unique,

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:13.280
realize that it was also drawn just as he saw it, and it all comes together!

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:13.820
What?

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:18.480
Ergo, Sensei's grandfather was a four-eyed alien!

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.040
When he drew this, the eyes on top were closed!

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:26.100
Wow, discovery of the century!

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.970
You nasty little brat!

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:30.690
Head Maid, what are you going to do about her?

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.180
Wait! If you think of it as more than just a painting...

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.090
That's it!

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:48.990
What is?

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:49.940
Are you okay?

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.700
Did your brain overheat or something?

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:58.090
A detective always laughs like that when they solve the mystery.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:08:59.240
Huh?

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:16.300
Now then, let's hear your answer.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.550
Pay attention, Sensei.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.710
I've got six playing cards here.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.830
How would I divide these between three people?

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:30.810
Are you mocking me?

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.220
You give two to each person, obviously!

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:34.330
Exactly.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.100
Okay then, how would you split this castella between three people?

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:39.960
Cut it into thirds, naturally.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:41.390
Bingo!

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.690
Oh crap, there's none for me!

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:44.880
What are you getting at!?

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.130
Those were hints.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:54.050
I hate how detective novels beat around the bush!

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.140
Quit giving me hints and just get to the point!

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.510
That was the last of them.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.170
If you connect the dots, you can solve the mystery.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.070
Just think about it, sir.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:10.330
Don't tell me you don't get it?

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:13.750
Well?

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:18.760
Why don't you understand!? It's
simple if you just use the formula!

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:21.170
How does this not make sense!?

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:25.020
So this is what it feels like!

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.590
This feels so good!

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.610
This must be how Sensei sees me!

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.250
Arashiyama-san.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.910
Arashiyama-san!

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.010
You better hurry up and tell him, or he's gonna lose it.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.510
How can Arashiyama understand something I can't?

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:42.260
Too much... this is too much...

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.970
Here, this is how I solved it!

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:54.960
W-What the?

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.360
This is the original form of the paintings!

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:02.900
It's a die.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.600
I drew the missing parts.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:10.570
The title “Eyes” refers to how he
thought of the dice as his own eyes.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:14.880
Each painting made up one face on the die.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.580
A fitting self-portrait for a gambler like your grandfather.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:22.910
But when the complete cube was divided between three people,

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.510
the work lost its meaning.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:30.110
What you ended up with was an odd pair of portraits!

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:32.210
And that's the truth behind them.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.230
What say you, my client?

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.540
Arashiyama-san actually looks smart for once!

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:43.900
It's a miracle!

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:47.910
Your body won't go soft no matter how much vinegar you drink.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.170
Eyes can say just as much as one's mouth.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:54.080
One's eyes will always speak the truth,

00:11:54.080 --> 00:12:00.340
but this rests on whether or not people's eyes can perceive the true meaning of these words.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.660
Upon discussing it with my relatives,

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:08.600
just like you said, we found the remaining 4 portraits.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:13.760
The work now assembled like my grandfather had intended,

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:16.720
I give to you as a token of my thanks.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:18.320
Hell no!

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:21.140
Don't taint this store more than you already have!

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:23.170
If we hang this here, it'll scare off the customers!

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:26.830
Hey, cut it out! This is a gift Sensei gave to me!

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.440
Then hang it in your own room!

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:31.180
Nah,

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.360
that'd be kinda weird.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.980
Poco means “a little bit” in Italian.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:47.950
Onions contain an allyl compound that causes hemoglobin in the blood to oxidize.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:51.310
When consumed by animals other than humans, it induces hemolytic anemia, which can be deadly.

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:58.430
However, in humans it is said to improve health, though there is insufficient medical data to corroborate this.

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:04.510
When an onion is cut, allyl sulfide is released, evaporating into the air and stimulating the production of tears.

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:09.960
But if chilled beforehand, the release of allyl sulfide can be prevented, and therefore will not induce tears.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.630
Such wisdom is part of the housewife's repertoire.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.630
Aren't you gonna ask me why I'm crying?

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:21.250
It's onions.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.510
Car-rots, on-ions. Now you try.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.940
Car-rots, on-ions.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.950
Car-rots, on-ions, ready, go!

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:37.990
I'm not a little kid! I can manage to go shopping by myself, you know.

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:39.750
You mean like last time?

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:43.010
You've got a history of forgetting. That's why we're doing this.

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:46.280
See? Oniorrot.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:49.180
If you forget on the way there, just remember that.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.190
You're really making me mad!

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:54.770
There's a limit to how much you can mock someone!

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.560
Onions and carrots. Who could screw that up?

00:13:58.560 --> 00:13:59.300
Huh?

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.780
It was “oniorrot”, so...

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:05.690
Yeah, round with a pointy part. That's right.

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:07.620
Hmm? Wait, was the round part supposed to mean potatoes?

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.620
Huh? Which one was it...

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:16.160
Pounding it into my head like that just makes me question myself more.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:24.840
Pearl! Pearl!

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:25.800
Pearl!

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.340
Darn it.

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:34.270
Whoa. A pretty boy.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:40.350
Eyes.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:41.420
Crossed eyes.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:42.140
Crying.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:42.930
Optimism.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.530
“When it rains, it pours.”

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.120
Eyes are the mirror that reflects our hearts.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:51.280
Thus, through people's eyes can we convey
our emotions, and understand one another.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:53.240
What're you looking at?

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:54.620
Your butt.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:56.030
What?

00:14:56.410 --> 00:15:01.420
Hmm, seeing as how his voice is cracking, he must be around 12 or 13.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:04.670
Dying his hair blond at this age will end up turning it gray.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.590
Hey, you. You're Hotori, right?

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:07.990
Huh?

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:11.590
I saw you plow into that cop on your scooter the other day.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:15.890
“Hey you?” Shouldn't you be calling me “Hotori-san” or “Onee-chan”?

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:19.770
The shopping district's pretty rough on kids who don't pay their dues.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:20.700
Oh yeah?

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.490
What are you doing, anyway?

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:25.220
You're acting awfully suspicious. You praying or something?

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:26.830
See for yourself.

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:33.030
It's a cat!

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.090
“Cat's eyes.”

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:38.620
A phrase used to describe someone of fickle character.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:41.270
It refers to a cat's pupils widening and narrowing at a moment's notice.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:50.220
Most people would argue that this phrase was actually inspired by a cat's ever-fickle dispositions.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:55.960
Also, because many cat owners are charmed by their cats' personalities,

00:15:55.960 --> 00:16:01.160
“cat's eyes” can also refer to someone who is devilishly charming.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:04.780
My cat won't come out from there.

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:07.660
Oh, that was her name?

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:08.220
Yup.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.320
Why would you name your cat Pearl?

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.630
Oh! Like “casting pearls before swine”, right?

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:14.900
You're pretty clever!

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:15.570
Thanks.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:17.460
I know. Here, use this oniorrot.

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:18.490
You mean onion?

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:19.310
You want some?

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:20.500
Why would I want an onion?

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:21.900
Not for you, for the cat!

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:24.860
I'm saying we could use this to lure her out.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:28.420
And I'm saying cats don't like onions.

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.630
Besides, it's bad for them.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:34.790
Oh, yeah. Doesn't it spook them or something?

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.500
You're thinking of squids.

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:38.270
Squid pro quo...

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.240
Squid pro quo.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:44.880
Go home already! It's freezing out here!

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:46.540
It's even snowing, dammit!

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:50.190
I'm sorry! I don't know what
possessed me to say something so stupid!

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:55.500
Pearl! I won't let her tell any more dumb jokes, so come out already!

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:56.820
This is my fault!?

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:58.990
Cat's don't like the cold.

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:00.620
Pearl!

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.810
Relax. She'll come out eventually.

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:05.620
Cats are just fickle like that, right?

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:08.110
My Pearl's a good girl, though.

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:10.250
Yeah, that's what every owner says.

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.570
It'd be a bad if I left her here and she got sick.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:17.800
Hey, you're a nicer person than you look.

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.520
W-What is it?

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:26.430
What the hell are you doing, dumbass!?

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:30.610
Y-You hit me!

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:32.780
'Cause you were trying to strangle me!

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:33.780
What the hell!?

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:37.690
Well, I was thinking if her owner's in trouble, she'd come save you.

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:39.280
She's not a dog!

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.140
I have a dog too, you know.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:43.190
And I'm sure it'd come save me from danger.

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:43.790
Probably.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:47.540
I told you. Listen, does that look like a dog to you?

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:52.440
Sheesh. Guess we have to get serious and think of a plan.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.470
Whatever, just take off, all right?

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:04.090
She came out!

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:08.400
See? I told you onions were the way to go!

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.770
Grab her, Hotori!

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:12.770
Right! Leave it all to Onee-chan!

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:15.340
Gotcha!

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.390
I call it “The Maid's Cage”!

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:25.280
That sounds like the name of a porno!

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:26.400
Nasty little brat.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:27.830
Meow!

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.130
Jeez, you had me worried sick!

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:32.500
Yay, good for you, Pearl!

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:34.270
Good for y—

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:40.890
No way! Did I step on her?

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.970
Do you see a tail-like thing lying around?

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.130
Are you by chance looking for her tail?

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:50.380
Uh, it wasn't my fault!

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:52.860
But it was an accident!
Okay, maybe it was,

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:54.480
It wasn't on purpose!

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.650
This little angel never had one to begin with.

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:57.320
Huh?

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.320
Cymric are a breed of cats that don't have tails.

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:04.810
Long ago, when her ancestors boarded Noah's ark,

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.960
the door closed on its tail and tore it off, or so the story goes.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:07.970
Thank god, I thought PETA was gonna have my head!
the door closed on its tail and tore it off, or so the story goes.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:11.880
Thank god, I thought PETA was gonna have my head!

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:13.970
It's a romantic legend don't you think?

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.440
But doesn't that story seem weird?

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:18.600
Pearl and her ancestors are different cats.

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:22.300
The dad's injury wouldn't be passed onto his kids, would it?

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:25.100
Who cares? It's just a legend.

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:29.130
'Kay then. Thanks anyhow!

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:29.850
Don't mention it!

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:32.680
Thanks anyhow, huh?

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:35.480
What a snotty little brat.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:37.820
Oh yeah, what's your name, anyway?

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:39.190
Huh?

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:43.780
Meh, cat boy works, I guess.

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:49.770
We should have taught her the “don't stop and smell the roses” song too.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:53.330
That, or at least drawn her a map.

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:02.120
Hotori!

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:05.650
Oh, you're the cat bo—

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.110
...girl?

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:12.890
Sorry! I thought you were a younger boy.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:16.590
I did think your voice was kinda odd.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:18.650
So you go here too, huh?

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:20.320
I knew the whole time, though.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:21.510
What class are you in?

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:22.300
Class A.

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:24.070
You don't say...

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:27.560
Hang on, I'm in class A too!

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.680
Was she always in my class? No, she wasn't!

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:32.580
I at least remember all of my classmates' names!

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:38.090
Crap! I figured I'd run into a ghost one day, but this is...

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:42.240
Speaking of which, she did suddenly disappear yesterday.

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:45.450
I bet if I look up now, <i>poof </i>! She'll be gone, or something!

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:49.470
She followed me all the way to school, which means I'm already doomed!

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.100
Mornin', Kon-Senpai!

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:54.630
Senpai?

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:58.020
Hey, Haribara. We're holding a
tournament today during club time.

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:04.840
This new rubber paddle will be the
reaper's scythe that claims your head!

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.030
You can talk trash <i>after </i> you beat me.

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:13.910
Oh my, I wasn't aware you were my senior, Senpai.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:16.120
You just look so young, that's all...

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:20.520
I'm terribly sorry if I offended you.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:25.600
There, there. Don't sweat it, “Onee-chan”!

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:28.970
Could we just drop that already?

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:03.860
Hip-hop isn't just rap.

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:05.700
“Maid.”

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:11.190
In modern Japan, the word refers to girls who labor dressed in the cloth of the English housekeeper.

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:13.740
Maid cafés, maid bars, maid massages, maid mahjong parlors,

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:15.760
all the way back to the original duty of cleaning.

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:21.150
Their momentum has carried them
through to today, with no signs of stopping.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:25.740
And now, here we have two girls battling it out with their maid spirit on the line!

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:35.260
Why are you doing this?

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:39.760
You wanna know? It's because of the maid's—

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:44.520
There's a curse that's been haunting people, one after the other.

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:45.670
Woah, that's scary!

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:49.490
The curse moves to a different person every minute.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:51.210
That's an awfully lenient curse.

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:55.450
Once the first victim is cursed, the
whole school follows in 10 minutes.

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:57.880
Now, what if you started with two victims?

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:00.160
How fast would the whole school fall under the curse?

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:02.870
Twice as many people, so... five minutes?

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:04.210
Wrong!

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:10.350
The world turned yesterday, too.
